  "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={6}^{u},\\,u=1-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={6}^{u}ln\u20616,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={6}^{u}ln\u20616\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {6}^{u}ln\u20616 \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln\u20616.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
